  "deliberation_state": "The room is now strictly divided between 4 5 7 (12 colleagues: 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 10, 11, 12, 15, 18, 19, 20) and 5 4 7 (9 colleagues: 2, 5, 7, 9, 13, 14, 16, 17, 21). All 21 colleagues are unanimous on Part (C) = 7 (Eugene Onegin). Both candidate sequences sum to 16 (a multiple of 8).",
  "critical_evidence_and_corrections": {
    "canto_one_fact_check": "Colleague 19 provided a decisive textual fact-check dismantling the entire premise of the 5 4 7 faction regarding Part (B). The 5 4 7 camp had argued that lines 25–28 interrupt an active scene of parental mourning/orphanhood, even citing a spurious line ('The world was new and yet we had survived'). Colleague 19 verified the actual lines (17–30): there is zero mention of parents or grief in this passage (parental death is not recounted until lines 69–74). Instead, the couplet directly glosses 'the darkest secret' (line 26), which throughout Pale Fire denotes mortality, the afterlife, and whether individual consciousness survives.",
    "part_a_analysis": "The prompt asks: in the story where the possibility of reversed footwear is explicitly mentioned, 'to whom would the reversed shoes have properly belonged?' In Doyle's 'The Adventure of the Empty House', Holmes states: 'I might, it is true, have reversed my boots... but the sight of three sets of tracks... would certainly have suggested a fraud.' The boots under consideration explicitly belonged to Holmes ('my boots'). Moran was never on the path, never wore reversed shoes, never contemplated reversing shoes, and was perched high above firing an air-gun. Attributing the shoes to Moran is canonically nonexistent.",
    "part_b_analysis": "The prompt requires an allusive act underscoring a theme 'in a way that an allusion to the other three stories in (A) would not.' The four stories are Study in Scarlet, Final Problem, Empty House, Priory School. Final Problem is the story of Holmes's death; Empty House is singularly renowned in world literature as Holmes's 'resurrection' / return from presumed death. This aligns uniquely with Shade's central inquiry into persistence after death (Option 5), whereas 'interrupted grief' (Option 4) relied on an erroneous/fabricated reading of Canto One."
  },
  "current_position": "4 5 7 is overwhelmingly supported by literal textual facts in both Doyle and Nabokov. In the next round, emphasize Colleague 19's fact-check regarding lines 25-28 ('the darkest secret' vs. the non-existent parental grief) to help consolidate consensus around 4 5 7."
